  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "Find the global maximum value of the function $f(x)=-{x}^{3}-6{x}^{2}-9x+3$ on the closed interval $-3\\le x\\le -1.$",
  "worked_solution": "Let's apply the candidates test step by step.\n\n**Step 1**: We find the critical points. Taking the derivative of $f(x)=-{x}^{3}-6{x}^{2}-9x+3,$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{\u2032}(x)=-3{x}^{2}-12x-9,\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{\u2032}(x)=0,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3{x}^{2}-12x-9 & =0 \\\\ -3({x}^{2}+4x+3) & =0 \\\\ -3(x+3)(x+1) & =0 \\\\ x & =-3,-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the critical points are $x=-3,-1,$ which coincide with the endpoints of the given interval.\n\n**Step 2**: We evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-3) & =-(-3)^{3}-6(-3)^{2}-9(-3)+3=3 \\\\ f(-1) & =-(-1)^{3}-6(-1)^{2}-9(-1)+3=7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We compare all of the values found in step $2.$\n\n- The greatest value is $f(-1)=7,$ so the global maximum is $7.$\n\n- The smallest value is $f(-3)=3,$ so the global minimum is $3.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061690/assets/2eca6b83a3f99ca87a70e381490e99c98e80a535cd3051a4cdd0740f82de577b.png)",
